# ============================================================
# flow/antenna_taper_passthru.buda
#
# Counter-configuration (b) for the per-bit ANTENNA audit's
# pass-through reach: THE TAPER.
#
# The intuition this exists to test is: "if a bit passes through
# a connected block, it must have another connection pulling it
# to the far side."  That holds per BUS.  It does NOT hold per
# BIT once the tree is per-bit tapered, because a far-side stub
# can belong to OTHER bits only.
#
# Here DIVERGENT bundles three buses that share one driver into
# a single fan-out tree, so the trunk carries all 20 bits while
# each branch carries its own:
#
#     a[8] -> r_near   branch taps the trunk EARLY
#     c[4] -> mid      the trunk passes THROUGH this block
#     b[8] -> r_far    branch at the far end
#
# An `a` bit's last electrical junction is its own branch, well
# before `mid`.  Its metal does not stop there: `mid` is a
# connected block the segment must cover, so adjust_bit_spans
# re-extends the bit to preserve that crossing (issue #496).
# The result is metal past the bit's last junction, justified
# only by a coverage CONTRACT — nothing joins the wire there.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY FOUND is sharper than the pass-through
# question it was built for.  Measured:
#
#   topo TRUNK_H@y100   seg_bits {0: 20 bits, 1: 8 bits}
#   bit 0  a_0  span=[200,700]  vias=[322,322]
#   ...
#   bit 7  a_7  span=[200,700]  vias=[338,338]
#   check_design dnuts: Success -- no violations
#
# The eight `a` bits go to r_near and their ONLY junction is
# that branch at x~330.  Their trunk metal runs to x=700 --
# r_far, a block they do not connect to.  ~370 units each,
# ~2960 in total, reaching nothing of theirs.
#
# The mechanism is not the crossing.  BUSTERM TAPS ARE PER
# SEGMENT WHILE BITS ARE PER BIT: the trunk taps drv at 200 and
# r_far at 700, and that tap vouches for every bit on the
# segment, including the eight that never go there.  #678's
# retraction does not fire either, because it snaps an end only
# when that end's conn resolves to a wire for THAT bit -- a
# busterm face is not a seg conn, so "ends with no endpoint conn
# keep the abstract span" applies.
#
# FIXED.  A tap now re-extends a bit only when that bit's own
# path terminates at the tapped block (Topology::seg_busterm_bits
# -> BusSegment::busterm_face_bits), and a tap that does not
# serve the bit counts as a stale END, so the retraction fires
# for it exactly as it does for a tapered-out conn.  On this
# vehicle the trunk's bit metal drops 10000 -> 7840 (-21.6%):
#
#   a_* (branch at ~330)   [200,700] -> [200,520]
#   c_* (to mid)           [200,700] -> [200,520]
#   b_* (to r_far)         [200,700]    unchanged, correctly
#
# The membership is read by THREE stages through ONE predicate
# (`seg_busterm_serves_bit`), because a tap the placer declines
# to reach must be a tap the audits decline to demand or to
# credit:
#
#   DetailedNUTS       retract past a tap that is not the bit's
#   check_dnuts        do not require the bit to reach it
#                      (read per SEGMENT it reported every
#                      retracted bit as a BUSTERM_OPEN)
#   ANTENNA (per bit)  do not credit its reach to the bit
#                      (read per SEGMENT it would excuse the
#                      very metal the retraction removes)
#
# The residual on a_* is NOT the tap: it is `mid`'s PASS-THROUGH
# coverage, the contract metal adjust_bit_spans re-extends to on
# purpose (issue #496).  Whether a crossing should vouch for a
# bit at all is the open question its sibling flow discusses;
# this vehicle keeps that stretch visible so the two are not
# confused.
#
# Its sibling flow/antenna_wide_bus_passthru.buda shows the
# OTHER route to this blind spot — a bus wider than the block it
# crosses — is unreachable: the block's edges are Hanan lines
# that pin the trunk's interval.  Every block on the trunk row
# here is deliberately given the SAME tall extent so that the
# interval stays wide enough to place the bus, isolating the
# taper as the only variable.
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/antenna_taper_passthru.buda --no-viz
# ============================================================

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33

corner_margin dx 20 dy 8

# Every block ON the trunk row shares y 100..200, so their edges
# contribute the SAME two Hanan lines and the trunk's interval
# stays 100 units wide — room for all 20 bits.
add_block drv    100 100  200 200
add_block mid    450 100  520 200
add_block r_far  700 100  800 200

# The early branch sits BELOW the row, so the trunk taps it with
# a stub rather than crossing it.
add_block r_near 300  20  360  60

# One driver, three destinations: DIVERGENT groups by the shared
# DRIVER, so these become ONE per-bit tapered fan-out tree.
add_bus a[8] drv.tx r_near.rx
add_bus c[4] drv.tx mid.rx
add_bus b[8] drv.tx r_far.rx

run_bundler DIVERGENT
generate_topologies
run_planner 1
run_nuts

def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

run_detailed_nuts
dump_topologies --conn
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
